---
// Volume set (CURRICULUM_GAPS_PLAN.md Section 6, item 2). Data: src/data/sets/volume-0.yaml. Not a topic.
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import SetTable, { type SetRow } from '../components/SetTable';
import { loadData, lessonHref, topicSlug } from '../lib/data';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const data = await loadData();
const set = data.sets['volume-0'];
const byId = new Map(data.curriculum.map((t) => [t.id, t]));
const num = (d: string) => (/^\d+$/.test(d) ? Number(d) : 0);
const rows: SetRow[] = [...set.problems]
  .sort((a, b) => byId.get(a.topic)!.order - byId.get(b.topic)!.order || num(a.difficulty) - num(b.difficulty) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id))
  .map((p) => {
    const t = byId.get(p.topic)!;
    return { id: p.id, title: p.title, url: p.url, source: p.source, difficulty: p.difficulty, summary: p.summary, apiOnly: p.source_check === 'api', topicLabel: `${t.id} ${t.title}`, topicHref: data.lessons[t.id] ? lessonHref(t) : undefined };
  });
const count = (s: string) => set.problems.filter((p) => p.source === s).length;
const recalled = set.problems.filter((p) => p.source_check === 'api').length;
---
<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Volume set', description: 'Easy problems for speed and clean code, with no new ideas.' }}>
  <p>
    This set has {set.problems.length} easy problems: {count('codeforces')} from Codeforces (rated 800 to 1000), {count('atcoder')} from AtCoder,
    {count('leetcode')} from LeetCode (Easy) and {count('cses')} from CSES, all at that level. <strong>None of them needs a new idea.</strong>
    Each one is here so you can practise the part of contest solving that is not a clever idea: reading fast, writing the obvious solution without bugs, and sending it.
  </p>
  <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Do a problem once the lesson in its last column is done.</strong> The column shows the earliest topic that gives everything the problem needs. Nothing in this set needs more than Phase 0 and Phase 1 topics.</li>
    <li><strong>Race a timer.</strong> Read the whole statement and the constraints, state the idea in one sentence, then code. If you are not near a first submission after about 15 minutes, the problem is not easy for you yet: read the lesson again instead of grinding on.</li>
    <li><strong>Write clean code the first time.</strong> Short functions, names that say what a value is, one clear loop instead of a clever one, no copied blocks. Clean code is faster to debug, and debugging time is where an easy problem becomes a slow one.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the usual traps before you submit:</strong> the largest input against the integer type, an array that must be reset between test cases, and the smallest input (n = 1). The <a href={url('/drills/')}>Implementation drills</a> train exactly these.</li>
    <li><strong>Log your minutes.</strong> The Log button records them. When the same kind of easy problem still takes more than 10 minutes, redo a drill and then redo the problem.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Which problems are used where: every problem here is used nowhere else on the site, so none of them is a ladder, checkpoint, review or exam problem and none spoils one.
    The <a href={url('/contest-strategy/')}>Contest strategy</a> page says what to do with the clock in a real contest.
    {recalled > 0 && <> For the {recalled} Codeforces problems (marked †) the statement could not be opened when the set was built, so their one-line summaries are recalled; links open the real statements.</>}
  </p>
  <SetTable client:load rows={rows} kind="volume" />
</StarlightPage>
